<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'

import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Exchange from '#src/lib/Exchange.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc
    seeAlso={[
        '/app/create-app',
        '/app/environment',
        '/app/on-config',
        '/app/on-start',
        '/middleware/middleware',
        '/app/on-principal',
        '/app/on-refusal',
        '/app/on-error',
        '/app/on-health',
        '/app/on-report',
        '/app/on-stop',
    ]}
>
    <Section title="A hook is a call in a handler file or a server script">
        <p>Booting the CRM imports every handler file and every
            <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code>, and each hook they call is filed at that
            file's route. <code>abide start</code> then boots the app and binds the port.
            <a href={url('/app/create-app')}><code>createApp</code></a> boots the same app with
            no port, so a test asks it through <code>app.fetch</code>.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import { expect, test } from 'bun:test'
            import { createApp } from '@abide/abide/server'

            test('the pool is connected before the first request', async () => {
                await using app = await createApp()
                const answer = await app.fetch(
                    new Request('http://crm.invalid/ops/getPool'),
                )
                const { connectedAt, upAt } = await answer.json()
                expect(connectedAt <= upAt).toBe(true)
            })
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Settings resolve during the boot, and the environment wins">
        <p><a href={url('/app/on-config')}><code>onConfig</code></a> gives a default, and a
            deployment's environment overrides it.
            <a href={url('/ambients/config')}><code>config</code></a> reads the result. It is
            resolved as the app boots rather than on the first request, so a setting a schema
            refuses keeps the app from starting. See
            <a href={url('/app/environment')}>Environment</a>.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import { config, onConfig, reactive, rpc } from '@abide/abide/server'

            onConfig(() => ({ RETRIES: 3 }))

            export default rpc(reactive(() => ({ retries: config.get().RETRIES })))
        `} />
        <p>The CRM's <code>.env</code> sets <code>RETRIES=5</code>. Its retry count, asked when
            this page loaded:</p>
        <Exchange path="/ops/getRetries" />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Connect the database before the first request, once per process">
        <p><a href={url('/app/on-start')}><code>onStart</code></a> wraps the boot. Work before
            <code>await start()</code> runs before the config is read and the routes are built,
            and work after it once they are. It runs once per process, not per request, and
            the port stays closed until it returns.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import { onStart, rpc } from '@abide/abide/server'
            import { pool } from './lib/pool.server.ts'

            onStart(async (start) => {
                await pool.connect()
                await start()
                pool.upAt = new Date().toISOString()
            })

            export default rpc(() => ({
                connectedAt: pool.connectedAt,
                upAt: pool.upAt,
            }))
        `} />
        <p>The CRM's pool, asked when this page loaded. It connected before the app came up,
            and both times are the boot's, not this request's:</p>
        <Exchange path="/ops/getPool" />
    </Section>

    <Section title="A request passes the middleware before the handler runs">
        <p>The root layout's <a href={url('/middleware/middleware')}><code>middleware</code></a>
            runs over every request before routing, and a handler's own runs after. A rung
            that refuses answers in the handler's place, so the handler never runs. The CRM's
            commission takes <a href={url('/middleware/signed-in')}><code>signedIn</code></a>.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import { rpc, signedIn } from '@abide/abide/server'

            export default rpc(() => ({ amount: '$6,200' }), {
                middleware: [signedIn],
            })
        `} />
        <p>Asked by nobody signed in, the rung answers <code>401</code> as Problem
            Details:</p>
        <Exchange path="/account/getCommission" />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Look up who is calling at most once per request">
        <p>The CRM's cookie holds only the member's id.
            <a href={url('/app/on-principal')}><code>onPrincipal</code></a> turns the id into the
            member the first time a request reads <code>principal</code>, and never again in
            that request. A caller with no cookie is anonymous and the hook does not run.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import { onPrincipal } from '@abide/abide/server'
            import { TEAM } from './lib/TEAM.server.ts'

            onPrincipal((claims) => {
                const { id } = claims as { id: string }
                const member = TEAM.find((one) => one.id === id)
                return member && { name: member.name, role: member.role }
            })
        `} />
        <p>The CRM's contact shows its deal value to an admin. Asked signed out, no role was
            resolved and the value is left out:</p>
        <Exchange path="/account/getContact" />
    </Section>

    <Section title="A refusal is an answer, so no error hook hears the refusal">
        <p>A handler that returns <a href={url('/helpers/refuse')}><code>refuse</code></a>
            answers with that status as Problem Details. Nothing threw, so
            <code>onError</code> stays quiet. The CRM's mailer is down, and its statement
            refuses <code>502</code>.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import { refuse } from '@abide/abide'
            import { rpc } from '@abide/abide/server'

            export default rpc(({ id }: { id: string }) =>
                refuse(502, 'the mailer refused the statement'),
             { methods: ['POST'] },)
        `} />
        <Exchange
            method="POST"
            path="/ops/sendStatement"
            body={'{ "id": "c2" }'}
         />
    </Section>

    <Section title="The browser hears every refusal through `onRefusal`">
        <p><a href={url('/app/on-refusal')}><code>onRefusal</code></a> runs in the browser, not
            on the server. It stays filed while the component that called it stands, and hears
            the refusal any call on the page gets. The CRM asks for a sign-in again on a
            <code>401</code>.</p>
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script>
            import { onRefusal, reactive } from '@abide/abide'

            let signedOut = reactive(false)

            onRefusal((refusal) => {
                if (refusal.status === 401) signedOut = true
            })
            </script>

            {#if signedOut}
                <p class="status">Your session ended. Sign in again.</p>
            {/if}
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="An unexpected throw reaches `onError`, and the caller gets a bare 500">
        <p><a href={url('/app/on-error')}><code>onError</code></a> in the companies layout's
            server script hears what a request under <code>/companies</code> throws, nearest
            route first. The caller learns nothing of the cause.</p>
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script server>
            import { onError } from '@abide/abide/server'
            import { tracker } from './lib/tracker.server.ts'

            onError((error) => tracker.capture(error))
            </script>

            <slot />
        `} />
        <p>The CRM's export throws. The tracker got the message, and the caller got
            this:</p>
        <Exchange method="POST" path="/companies/exportCompanies" body={'{}'} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="The health check runs `onHealth` on every poll">
        <p><a href={url('/app/on-health')}><code>onHealth</code></a> runs each time
            <code>/__abide/health</code> is asked, so it reports the pool as it is now. Its
            fields are merged over abide's baseline. See
            <a href={url('/operating/health')}><code>health</code></a>.</p>
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script server>
            import { onHealth } from '@abide/abide/server'
            import { pool } from './pool.server.ts'

            onHealth(() => ({ database: pool.connected ? 'connected' : 'closed' }))
            </script>
        `} />
        <Exchange path="/__abide/health" />
    </Section>

    <Section title="A problem report a person sends reaches `onReport`">
        <p>A person sends a report from the browser with
            <a href={url('/operating/report')}><code>report</code></a>, and the server hands it
            to <a href={url('/app/on-report')}><code>onReport</code></a>, its secrets taken out.
            With no hook filed, the send is refused <code>501</code>. The CRM keeps each
            summary for the browser that sent it.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import { principal } from '@abide/abide'
            import { onReport } from '@abide/abide/server'

            const received = new Map<string, string[]>()

            onReport((report) => {
                const caller = principal.caller()
                const sent = received.get(caller) ?? []
                received.set(caller, [...sent, report.summary])
            })
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Close the database pool after the last request drains">
        <p><a href={url('/app/on-stop')}><code>onStop</code></a> runs once, on
            <code>SIGTERM</code>, <code>Ctrl-C</code> or <code>App.stop</code>, after the app
            stops taking requests and those in flight are answered. It mirrors
            <code>onStart</code>: <code>await stop()</code> runs the hooks filed after yours
            first.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import { onStop } from '@abide/abide/server'
            import { pool } from './lib/pool.server.ts'

            onStop(async (stop) => {
                await stop()
                await pool.close()
            })
        `} />
    </Section>
</Doc>
